From c80ed3cc77435ac86eb7c1fe5231279937d146a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 07:58:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/31] hir_typeck: defer inherent methods that only match by
 binding receiver ivars

When a method call's receiver still contains inference variables, an
inherent impl whose self type is more specific than the receiver (e.g.
`impl<T> Either<T, T>` seen through `Either<Iter<T>, _>`, or
`impl GetSpan<Span>` seen through `GetSpan<_>`) only matches by binding
those ivars. Committing to it immediately pins the ivars, even though the
expression that would have resolved them (a pending method call, a match
arm) hasn't been processed yet, and the real type may select a trait
method or an inherent method at a deeper autoderef level instead.

rustc doesn't have this problem because it checks the earlier expressions
first. Mirror that: outside fallback mode, when the matched inherent impl
compares Fuzzy against the receiver, leave the node for a later revisit.
Fallback mode (nothing else can make progress) commits as before, so
`W(1).go()` with `impl W<u8>` and `Either::Left(dflt()).into_inner()`
still resolve through the inherent impl.

Hit by rayon 1.10.0 iter/extend.rs:62
    let strs = match vecs {
        Either::Left(vec) => Either::Left(vec.iter()),
        Either::Right(list) => Either::Right(list.iter().flatten()),
    };
    strs.map(AsRef::as_ref)...
where `either`'s inherent `impl<T> Either<T, T>::map` was chosen through
`Either<slice::Iter<T>, _>` before `.flatten()` resolved the right arm:
"Type mismatch between slice::iter::Iter<T> and Flatten<...>".
And by ref-cast-impl 1.0.23 (quote_spanned!), where quote's autoref
specialisation `get_span(span).__into_span()` picked `impl GetSpan<Span>`
through `GetSpan<_>` before the argument type (DelimSpan) was known:
"Type mismatch between Span and DelimSpan".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
